1	// 02 · The 8-Second Filter: what fast filtering means for how we design messages.
2	// Composition: hook = one big question, nothing else; takeaway = two columns split by a
3	// vertical rule (line | question); alignment = left.
4	
5	module.exports = {
6	  num: 2,
7	  slug: "the-8-second-filter",
8	  title: "The 8-Second Filter",
9	  category: "Contextual Engineering & Education Delivery",
10	  palette: "baylor",
11	  hero: { layout: "survival-curve", concept: "A 'still reading' curve that drops steeply in the first seconds, then flattens: people screen fast, then stay" },
12	  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "split-columns", align: "left" },
13	  oneIdea: "Attention isn't short; it's filtered. Win the first line and people give you the rest.",
14	  question: "Would the first line of your last message to athletes pass the filter?",
15	  caveats: `Don't present the 8-second figure as fact, even ironically; the slide shows it to retire it. Claims that "Gen Z can't focus" are mostly marketing copy. The filtering pattern applies to everyone, and the same athletes will watch a long film session when it is worth their time. The curve on slide 3 is a schematic of the shape Liu et al. describe, not a plot of their data, and it is labeled that way.`,
16	  sources: [
17	    { id: "maybin2017", cite: "Maybin S. Busting the attention span myth. BBC News. 2017. https://www.bbc.com/news/health-38896790", grade: "investigative journalism", found: "external", supports: "The 8-second figure appears in the Microsoft Canada report but is cited to Statistic Brain; Statistic Brain's listed sources (NCBI, Associated Press) could find no record of the research." },
18	    { id: "microsoft2015", cite: "Microsoft Canada Consumer Insights. Attention spans. Microsoft Canada; 2015.", grade: "industry report", found: "external (as described by Maybin 2017)", supports: "The report that popularised '8 seconds, down from 12 in 2000'." },
19	    { id: "bradbury2016", cite: "Bradbury NA. Attention span during lectures: 8 seconds, 10 minutes, or more? Adv Physiol Educ. 2016;40(4):509-513. doi:10.1152/advan.00109.2016", grade: "narrative review", found: "external", supports: "The common 10-15 minute lecture attention limit rests on weak evidence; attention depends on the teacher and the material." },
20	    { id: "liu2010", cite: "Liu C, White RW, Dumais S. Understanding web browsing behaviors through Weibull analysis of dwell time. In: Proceedings of the 33rd International ACM SIGIR Conference. 2010:379-386. doi:10.1145/1835449.1835513", grade: "large observational log study", found: "external", supports: "Browsing shows 'negative aging': pages must pass an initial screening, after which the chance of leaving drops." },
21	    { id: "nielsen2011", cite: "Nielsen J. How long do users stay on web pages? Nielsen Norman Group. 2011. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/how-long-do-users-stay-on-web-pages/", grade: "practitioner summary of Liu et al. 2010", found: "external", supports: "Users often leave a page in 10-20 seconds; pages that communicate their value quickly hold attention much longer." },
22	  ],
23	  slides: [
24	    {
25	      role: "hook", time: 45,
26	      notes: `You have probably heard this one: the average attention span is now eight seconds, one second shorter than a goldfish. It has been in keynotes, books and staff trainings, and it gets used to justify a lot of decisions about how we talk to athletes.
27	
28	Before we build anything on it, let's check where it came from.`,
29	      build(s) {
30	        s.headline("Is your attention span shorter than a goldfish’s?", { y: 1.6, w: 10.6, h: 2.8, size: 76 });
31	        s.lead("The claim you’ve heard: 8 seconds, down from 12, one less than a goldfish.", { y: 4.75, w: 8.6, h: 1.1, size: 24 });
32	        s.source("Popularized by a Microsoft Canada report, 2015", { y: 5.95, w: 8, refs: ["microsoft2015"] });
33	      },
34	    },
35	    {
36	      role: "problem", time: 60,
37	      notes: `Here is the problem. The BBC traced the figure. It appears in the Microsoft Canada report, but the report cites a statistics website for it, not its own research. When the reporter contacted the sources that website listed, neither could find any record of the study.
38	
39	And it is not just the goldfish. The rule many of us learned, that people tune out of a lecture after ten to fifteen minutes, also turns out to rest on thin evidence. Attention depends heavily on who is teaching and what is being taught.
40	
41	So the premise is shaky. That doesn't mean attention is unlimited. It means we have been solving the wrong problem.`,
42	      build(s) {
43	        s.headline("Those attention numbers have no study behind them.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.8, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
44	        const rows = [
45	          ["The report cited a stats website, not its own research.", "Maybin, BBC 2017"],
46	          ["That website's sources had no record of the study.", "Maybin, BBC 2017"],
47	          ["Even the ‘10-minute lecture limit’ rests on thin evidence.", "Bradbury 2016"],
48	        ];
49	        rows.forEach(([t, src], i) => {
50	          const y = 2.6 + i * 1.22;
51	          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
52	          s.text(0.75, y + 0.2, 9.4, 0.95, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
53	          s.source(src, { x: 10.2, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.38, h: 0.45, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top" });
54	        });
55	        s.line(0.75, 6.26, 12.58, 6.26, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
56	        s.cite("maybin2017", "bradbury2016");
57	      },
58	    },
59	    {
60	      role: "insight", time: 70,
61	      notes: `What is real is a filter. Researchers who studied millions of web visits found that people make a fast call on whether something is worth their time. Lots of visits end in the first ten to twenty seconds. But a page that gets past that first screening holds people much longer, and the longer they stay, the less likely they are to leave.
62	
63	That is the curve here. Steep drop at the start, then a long, flat tail. Attention isn't short. It's filtered.
64	
65	The same athlete who ignores a wall of text will sit through a long film session, because they already know it is worth it.`,
66	      build(s) {
67	        s.headline("Attention isn’t short. It’s filtered.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
68	        const ox = 1.3, oy = 5.55, w = 10.9, h = 2.85;
69	        s.rect(ox, oy - h, 1.7, h, { fill: "soft" });
70	        s.line(ox, oy, ox + w, oy, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
71	        s.line(ox, oy, ox, oy - h, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
72	        const pts = [[0, 1], [0.04, 0.72], [0.09, 0.5], [0.16, 0.38], [0.28, 0.31], [0.45, 0.27], [0.65, 0.245], [0.85, 0.23], [1, 0.222]];
73	        s.path(pts.map(([px, py]) => [ox + px * w, oy - py * h]), { smooth: true, color: "shape", w: 5 });
74	        s.text(ox + 0.12, oy - 0.55, 1.5, 0.45, "The filter", { font: "condBold", size: 20, color: "accent" });
75	        s.text(ox + w - 6, oy - h * 0.235 - 0.62, 6, 0.45, "Past the filter, people stay", { font: "cond", size: 20, color: "ink", align: "right" });
76	        s.text(ox, oy - h - 0.45, 4, 0.38, "Share still reading", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted" });
77	        s.text(ox, oy + 0.12, 4, 0.4, "Time on the message →", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted" });
78	        s.text(ox + w - 4, oy + 0.12, 4, 0.4, "Illustrative shape, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
79	        s.source("Pattern: Liu, White & Dumais 2010 · 10–20 second exits: Nielsen 2011", { refs: ["liu2010", "nielsen2011"] });
80	      },
81	    },
82	    {
83	      role: "application", time: 65,
84	      notes: `So design for the filter. Put the point in the first line, the thing that changes what someone does. Everything else is supporting detail for the people who keep reading.
85	
86	Here is a real-shaped example of a team message. The first version makes people read three lines to find out whether it matters to them. The second gives them the change, the time and the one action in the first line.
87	
88	This works for texts, emails, the whiteboard in the locker room, and the first slide of a presentation.`,
89	      build(s) {
90	        s.headline("Put the point in the first line.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
91	        s.label("Before", { x: 0.75, y: 2.6, w: 2 });
92	        s.text(0.75, 3.0, 11.6, 1.2, "“Hey team, a few reminders for this week since the schedule is shifting around a bit…”", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "muted", lineH: 1.2 });
93	        s.line(0.75, 4.35, 12.58, 4.35, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
94	        s.label("After", { x: 0.75, y: 4.6, w: 2, color: "accent" });
95	        s.text(0.75, 5.0, 11.6, 1.2, "“Thursday lift moves to 6:30 a.m. Bring water.”", { font: "medium", size: 32, color: "ink", lineH: 1.1 });
96	        s.text(0.75, 5.95, 11.6, 0.4, "Example message written for this deck.", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
97	      },
98	    },
99	    {
