// 16 · Reframing Anxiety as Excitement: same body, different story.
// Composition: hook = one word at 104pt with a narrow lead; takeaway = line top-left,
// question flush right in the lower half; alignment = left.

module.exports = {
  num: 16,
  slug: "reframing-anxiety-as-excitement",
  title: "Reframing Anxiety as Excitement",
  category: "Mental Health & Performance Psychology",
  palette: "slate",
  hero: { layout: "one-signal-two-labels", concept: "One arousal signal in the center (racing heart, quick breath) with two arrows to two readings of it: threat ('I'm not ready') or challenge ('I'm ready for this')" },
  compose: { hook: "single-word", takeaway: "question-right", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Pre-game nerves and excitement feel much the same in the body. Calling it excitement is easier than trying to calm down, and it tends to help a little.",
  question: "What do we say to athletes in the last minute before they compete?",
  caveats: `Keep this in its lane: everyday pre-performance nerves, not anxiety disorders. Anxiety that lingers, shows up away from competition, or gets in the way of sleep, school or relationships goes to Baylor's licensed mental health staff; a reframe is not treatment. The effect is real but small: the 2024 meta-analysis of 44 effect sizes found d = 0.23 and called these interventions "not silver bullets". Brooks (2014), Jamieson et al. (2012) and Moore et al. (2015) are lab studies with roughly 50-150 participants per study, mostly students, not athletes in competition. Anxiety and excitement share much the same high-arousal body response; don't say they are identical. Williams et al. (2010) is about imagery producing challenge or threat states, not about reappraisal itself; it supports the two-readings picture. The before/after lines on slide 4 were written for this deck.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "brooks2014", cite: "Brooks AW. Get excited: reappraising pre-performance anxiety as excitement. J Exp Psychol Gen. 2014;143(3):1144-1158. doi:10.1037/a0035325", grade: "series of lab experiments", found: "external", supports: "Most people believe calming down is the best way to cope with pre-performance anxiety; across karaoke singing, public speaking and math, people who reappraised anxiety as excitement (saying 'I am excited' or being told 'get excited') felt more excited and performed better than those who tried to calm down." },
    { id: "jamieson2012", cite: "Jamieson JP, Nock MK, Mendes WB. Mind over matter: reappraising arousal improves cardiovascular and cognitive responses to stress. J Exp Psychol Gen. 2012;141(3):417-422. doi:10.1037/a0025719", grade: "randomized lab experiment", found: "external", supports: "People told that stress arousal is functional and aids performance showed a more challenge-like cardiovascular response (higher cardiac output, lower vascular resistance) during a stressful speech, and less attentional bias to negative words." },
    { id: "moore2015", cite: "Moore LJ, Vine SJ, Wilson MR, Freeman P. Reappraising threat: how to optimize performance under pressure. J Sport Exerc Psychol. 2015;37(3):339-343. doi:10.1123/jsep.2014-0186", grade: "randomized lab experiment (n = 50), motor task", found: "external", supports: "In a pressurized golf-putting task, arousal reappraisal shifted participants from a threat-like to a more challenge-like cardiovascular response, and the reappraisal group outperformed controls." },
    { id: "williams2010", cite: "Williams SE, Cumming J, Balanos GM. The use of imagery to manipulate challenge and threat appraisal states in athletes. J Sport Exerc Psychol. 2010;32(3):339-358. doi:10.1123/jsep.32.3.339", grade: "lab experiment with athletes", found: "Zotero", supports: "Athletes can be moved into challenge or threat appraisals of the same stressful situation (here through imagery scripts), with different psychological and cardiovascular responses." },
    { id: "bosshard2024", cite: "Bosshard M, Gomez P. Effectiveness of stress arousal reappraisal and stress-is-enhancing mindset interventions on task performance outcomes: a meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials. Sci Rep. 2024;14(1):7923. doi:10.1038/s41598-024-58408-w", grade: "meta-analysis of randomized trials", found: "external", supports: "44 effect sizes: a small overall improvement in task performance (d = 0.23), somewhat larger for public performance tasks; 'not silver bullets' but a promising low-cost approach." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Think about the tunnel before a game, or the last minute before a big lift or a first start. Heart pounding, breathing quick, hands a little shaky.

Most of us have one line for that moment: calm down. Relax, take a breath, don't be nervous. It's what nearly everyone believes works. In one set of studies, a large majority of people said trying to calm down was the best way to handle nerves before a performance.

Today is about a different line, and why it may work better.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.75, 11.8, 1.8, "Butterflies.", { font: "condBold", size: 104, color: "accent", lineH: 1.0, role: "headline" });
        s.lead("A racing heart and quick breath before a big moment. Most of us tell athletes to calm down.", { y: 3.85, w: 6.0, h: 1.4, size: 24 });
        s.source("Brooks, J Exp Psychol Gen, 2014", { y: 5.95, w: 6, refs: ["brooks2014"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here's the problem with "calm down". Nerves are a high-energy state. Calm is a low-energy one. Asking someone to get from one to the other in the last sixty seconds is a big jump, and most people can't make it on command.

Excitement is also high energy. So the switch from nervous to excited is much smaller: you keep the energy and change what you call it.

That's what Alison Wood Brooks tested at Harvard. People about to sing, give a speech, or take a math test either tried to calm down or said "I am excited." The excited group felt more excited and performed better each time.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("“Calm down” asks for the hardest switch.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Nervous to calm", "A big jump, from high energy to low."],
          ["Nervous to excited", "A small one. Keep the energy, change the label."],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const y = 2.75 + i * 1.45;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.label(k, { x: 0.75, y: y + 0.4, w: 3.4, size: 16 });
          s.text(4.3, y + 0.28, 8.2, 1.0, t, { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.12 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 5.65, 12.58, 5.65, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.source("Brooks 2014: singing, public speaking and math tasks", { y: 5.95, w: 9, size: 13, refs: ["brooks2014"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `Here's why it works. The signal in the middle is the same: racing heart, quick breath. What changes is the story we attach to it.

Read it as "I'm not ready" and the body leans toward a threat response: blood vessels tighten and the heart works less efficiently. Read it as "I'm ready for this" and it leans toward a challenge response: more blood pumped with less resistance. That shift showed up in lab studies of stressful speeches and in a pressured golf-putting task, where the reappraisal group also putted better.

One honest note. Pooled across 44 results, the average gain is small. This is a useful nudge, not a cure-all.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Same body. Two stories.", { y: 1.2, w: 9, h: 0.75, size: 40 });
        const cx = 6.667, cy = 3.95, r = 1.0;
        s.circle(cx, cy, r, { fill: "soft", line: "muted", lineW: 1.25 });
        s.text(cx - 0.95, cy - 0.42, 1.9, 0.85, "Racing heart,\nquick breath", { font: "cond", size: 20, color: "ink", align: "center", valign: "middle", lineH: 1.05 });
        s.line(cx - r - 0.12, cy, 4.05, cy, { color: "muted", w: 1.5, arrow: "end" });
        s.line(cx + r + 0.12, cy, 9.28, cy, { color: "accent", w: 2.5, arrow: "end" });
        // Threat reading (left)
        s.label("Threat", { x: 0.75, y: cy - 0.95, w: 3.15, align: "right", size: 15 });
        s.text(0.75, cy - 0.55, 3.15, 0.55, "“I’m not ready.”", { font: "light", size: 26, color: "ink", align: "right" });
        s.text(0.75, cy + 0.1, 3.15, 0.85, "Vessels tighten, the heart works harder for less", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "right", lineH: 1.15 });
        // Challenge reading (right)
        s.label("Challenge", { x: 9.43, y: cy - 0.95, w: 3.15, size: 15, color: "accent" });
        s.text(9.43, cy - 0.55, 3.15, 0.55, "“I’m ready for this.”", { font: "medium", size: 26, color: "accent" });
        s.text(9.43, cy + 0.1, 3.15, 0.85, "More blood pumped, less resistance", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted", lineH: 1.15 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.45, 11.83, 0.45, "A small, real effect. A nudge, not a cure-all.", { font: "light", size: 20, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.source("Jamieson et al. 2012 · Moore et al. 2015 · Williams et al. 2010 · Pooled effect: Bosshard & Gomez 2024 · Simplified diagram", { x: 0.75, w: 11.83, align: "center", refs: ["jamieson2012", "moore2015", "williams2010", "bosshard2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So what do we actually say? Swap the "calm down" line for one that keeps the energy and names it as useful. Something like: "That's your body getting ready. Use it." Athletes can say it to themselves too; in the studies, simply saying "I am excited" out loud was enough to shift how people felt.

This works for coaches in the tunnel, Athletic Performance staff before a max test, athletic trainers on a return-to-play day.

One boundary. This is for everyday nerves before a performance. If an athlete's anxiety lingers, shows up away from competition, or gets in the way of sleep or school, that's a conversation for Baylor's licensed mental health staff.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Keep the energy. Change the label.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.label("Instead of", { x: 0.75, y: 2.55, w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.95, 11.6, 0.6, "“Calm down. Relax. Don’t be nervous.”", { font: "light", size: 26, color: "muted" });
        s.label("Try", { x: 0.75, y: 3.85, w: 3, color: "accent" });
        s.text(0.75, 4.25, 11.6, 0.65, "“That’s your body getting ready. Use it.”", { font: "medium", size: 34, color: "ink" });
        s.source("Example lines written for this deck", { y: 4.98, w: 8, size: 13, valign: "top" });
        s.text(0.75, 5.6, 11.6, 0.75, "Nerves that linger or disrupt daily life go to Baylor’s licensed mental health staff.", { font: "light", size: 20, color: "ink", lineH: 1.15 });
        s.cite("brooks2014");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 50,
      notes: `The takeaway: don't fight the butterflies. Name them. The energy is going to be there either way; what we call it is the part we can change.

Question for the room: what do we say to athletes in the last minute before they compete? Coaches, athletic trainers, Athletic Performance, anyone who is in the tunnel or on the sideline. If we each wrote down our usual line, how many of them would be some version of "calm down"?`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Don’t fight the butterflies. Name them.", { y: 1.35, w: 8.2, h: 2.2, size: 60 });
        s.text(5.7, 4.3, 6.88, 1.7, "What do we say to athletes in the last minute before they compete?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", align: "right", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

