  category: "Mental Health & Performance Psychology",
  palette: "night",
  hero: { layout: "three-step-loop", concept: "Three small nodes in a row (breathe, cue word, act) with a return line from act back to breathe: the same reset, repeated after every mistake" },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "centered", align: "center" },
  oneIdea: "A reset after a mistake is a skill: one slow breath, one cue word the athlete chose, the next action. It works when it's practiced before it's needed.",
  question: "Where in practice could athletes rehearse a reset after a mistake?",
  caveats: `Be straight about the evidence. Centering as a named technique rests on small studies; the one cited here is a single-subject design with 10- and 11-year-old basketball players (Haddad & Tremayne 2009). The stronger support is for the parts: pre-performance routines in general (meta-analysis, small effect in pre-post designs and moderate-to-large effects in experiments, including under pressure; Rupprecht et al.) and instructional or motivational self-talk (meta-analysis, moderate average effect; Hatzigeorgiadis et al. 2011). The slow-breathing review (Zaccaro et al. 2018) covers 15 small studies in healthy adults, not athletes in competition. Present this as a performance skill, not a mental health intervention: an athlete whose worry or panic doesn't settle, or shows up away from sport, should be connected with Baylor's licensed mental health staff.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "haddad2009", cite: "Haddad K, Tremayne P. The effects of centering on the free-throw shooting performance of young athletes. Sport Psychol. 2009;23(1):118-136. doi:10.1123/tsp.23.1.118", grade: "single-subject multiple-baseline design, young athletes", found: "external", supports: "A centering breath improved free-throw shooting for all participants, to varying degrees, in 10-11-year-old representative basketball players." },
    { id: "rupprecht2024", cite: "Rupprecht AGO, Tran US, Gröpel P. The effectiveness of pre-performance routines in sports: a meta-analysis. Int Rev Sport Exerc Psychol. 2024;17(1):39-64. doi:10.1080/1750984X.2021.1944271", grade: "meta-analysis (112 effect sizes)", found: "external", supports: "Pre-performance routines improved performance: small effect in pre-post designs (SMC 0.31), moderate-to-large in experiments under low pressure (g 0.64) and under pressure (g 0.70); not moderated by type of routine, age or skill level." },
    { id: "hatzi2011", cite: "Hatzigeorgiadis A, Zourbanos N, Galanis E, Theodorakis Y. Self-talk and sports performance: a meta-analysis. Perspect Psychol Sci. 2011;6(4):348-356. doi:10.1177/1745691611413136", grade: "meta-analysis (32 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Self-talk, including short cue words, had a moderate positive average effect on sport performance (ES = .48)." },
    { id: "zaccaro2018", cite: "Zaccaro A, Piarulli A, Laurino M, et al. How breath-control can change your life: a systematic review on psycho-physiological correlates of slow breathing. Front Hum Neurosci. 2018;12:353. doi:10.3389/fnhum.2018.00353", grade: "systematic review (15 studies, healthy adults)", found: "external", supports: "Slow breathing was associated with higher heart rate variability and with more relaxation and alertness and less anxiety and arousal." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Start with a moment everyone in this room has watched. A dropped pass, a missed free throw, a false start, a bad rep in front of the team.

Now think about the next five seconds. What does the athlete actually do? Most of the time, nothing on purpose. They replay the mistake, look at the bench, or start thinking about the score.

We coach almost every physical skill they have. Today is about one mental one: the reset.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("What happens in the five seconds after a mistake?", { x: 0.75, y: 2.1, w: 11.83, h: 2.6, size: 76, align: "center" });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 55,
      notes: `Here's the problem. After a mistake, attention goes everywhere except the next play: back to what just went wrong, ahead to the scoreboard, over to the coach. We tell athletes to "refocus" or "flush it", but we rarely teach them how.

Refocusing is a skill like any other. The research on routines, the short sequences athletes run before a free throw or a serve, says they help. A meta-analysis of more than a hundred results found routines improved performance, including in pressured conditions. And the type of routine mattered less than having one.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.9, 0, 4.433, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("After a mistake, attention goes everywhere but the next play.", { y: 1.25, w: 7.4, h: 2.4, size: 48 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.15, 7.2, 1.5, "Back to the error. Ahead to the score. “Refocus” is easy to say and rarely taught.", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "muted", lineH: 1.22 });
        s.text(9.35, 2.0, 3.25, 3.2, "Routines help, under pressure too. The kind of routine matters less than having one.", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "ink", lineH: 1.22 });
        s.source("Rupprecht, Tran & Gröpel, meta-analysis", { x: 9.35, y: 5.95, w: 3.3, size: 12, refs: ["rupprecht2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `A centering cue is about the simplest routine there is. Three steps, the same way every time.

Breathe: one slow, deliberate breath. Slow breathing is linked with feeling calmer and more alert at the same time. Cue word: one short word the athlete picked that points to the next action, like "smooth" or "through". Short cue words are a kind of self-talk, and self-talk has a moderate average effect on performance across studies. Act: the next play, the next rep.

