        s.text(0.75, 4.15, 7.2, 1.5, "Back to the error. Ahead to the score. “Refocus” is easy to say and rarely taught.", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "muted", lineH: 1.22 });
        s.text(9.35, 2.0, 3.25, 3.2, "Routines help, under pressure too. The kind of routine matters less than having one.", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "ink", lineH: 1.22 });
        s.source("Rupprecht, Tran & Gröpel, meta-analysis", { x: 9.35, y: 5.95, w: 3.3, size: 12, refs: ["rupprecht2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `A centering cue is about the simplest routine there is. Three steps, the same way every time.

Breathe: one slow, deliberate breath. Slow breathing is linked with feeling calmer and more alert at the same time. Cue word: one short word the athlete picked that points to the next action, like "smooth" or "through". Short cue words are a kind of self-talk, and self-talk has a moderate average effect on performance across studies. Act: the next play, the next rep.

And then the loop closes. Next mistake, same reset. The point is that it becomes automatic, so it is there when the pressure is highest.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("A reset is three small steps, done the same way every time.", { x: 1.2, y: 1.2, w: 10.93, h: 1.95, size: 54, align: "center" });
        const y = 4.35, r = 0.6;
        const xs = [4.1, 6.667, 9.233];
        const names = ["Breathe", "Cue word", "Act"];
        xs.forEach((x, i) => {
          s.circle(x, y, r, { line: i === 1 ? "accent" : "muted", lineW: i === 1 ? 2.25 : 1.25 });
          s.text(x - 0.95, y - 0.24, 1.9, 0.48, names[i], { font: "cond", size: 20, color: i === 1 ? "accent" : "ink", align: "center", valign: "middle" });
        });
        s.line(xs[0] + r + 0.1, y, xs[1] - r - 0.12, y, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, arrow: "end" });
        s.line(xs[1] + r + 0.1, y, xs[2] - r - 0.12, y, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, arrow: "end" });
        // Return path: act back to breathe, under the row.
        s.path([[xs[2], y + r + 0.08], [xs[2] - 0.35, y + 1.05], [6.667, y + 1.2], [xs[0] + 0.35, y + 1.05], [xs[0] + 0.05, y + r + 0.2]], { smooth: true, color: "muted", w: 1.25, dash: "dash" });
        s.line(xs[0] + 0.1, y + r + 0.28, xs[0] + 0.02, y + r + 0.1, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, arrow: "end" });
        s.text(4.67, y + 1.25, 4, 0.4, "Next mistake, same reset", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.source("Centering breath: Haddad & Tremayne 2009 · Cue words: Hatzigeorgiadis et al. 2011 · Slow breathing: Zaccaro et al. 2018", { x: 0.75, w: 11.83, align: "center", refs: ["haddad2009", "hatzi2011", "zaccaro2018"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `How do we build it? Three steps, and here the order matters.

First, the athlete picks the cue word. It should be theirs, short, and point at the next action, not at the mistake. Our licensed mental health and performance staff can help athletes who want to build this properly.

Second, rehearse it in practice, after every mistake, until it is boring. A reset that is only used on game day is a new skill under the most pressure.

Third, use the same reset in competition. Same breath, same word.

One honest note on the evidence: routines and self-talk have solid reviews behind them. Centering as a named technique has only small studies.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Build it in practice, not on game day.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["1", "The athlete picks the cue word."],
          ["2", "Rehearse the reset after every mistake in practice."],
          ["3", "Use the same reset in competition."],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const y = 2.45 + i * 0.95;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.16, 0.9, 0.7, k, { font: "cond", size: 34, color: "muted", lineH: 0.95 });
          s.text(1.75, y + 0.2, 10.8, 0.6, t, { font: "light", size: 28, color: "ink", lineH: 1.1 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 5.3, 12.58, 5.3, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.45, 11.8, 0.45, "Routines and self-talk have solid reviews behind them. Centering itself has only small studies.", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Rupprecht et al. · Hatzigeorgiadis et al. 2011 · Haddad & Tremayne 2009", { y: 6.0, w: 9, size: 12, refs: ["rupprecht2024", "hatzi2011", "haddad2009"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 50,
      notes: `The takeaway: practice the reset before you need it. Breathe, cue, go, the same way every time, until it shows up on its own.

Question for the room: where in practice could athletes rehearse a reset after a mistake? Coaches will have the best answers here: the drill where mistakes happen most, the scrimmage, the conditioning block. Athletic Performance and athletic trainers see mistakes and frustration too, in the weight room and in rehab.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Practice the reset before you need it.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.7, w: 11.83, h: 1.2, size: 64, align: "center" });
        s.line(6.167, 3.45, 7.167, 3.45, { color: "rule", w: 1.5 });
        s.text(1.9, 3.85, 9.53, 1.3, "Where in practice could athletes rehearse a reset after a mistake?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", align: "center", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

