  caveats: `The phrase pairs on slide 3 were written for this deck; they illustrate the MHFA principle of listening nonjudgmentally, not tested scripts. Gulliver et al. is a small qualitative study (15 young elite athletes). The Dazzi review covers research and clinical settings; its finding is that asking about suicide did not increase suicidal ideation, which supports asking directly, not staff doing assessment. Staff are not counselors: the job is to listen, ask, and connect to Baylor's licensed mental health staff, and to follow the department's emergency action plan. Safe messaging: keep any discussion of suicide calm and practical, never discuss methods, and always pair it with help (immediate danger: 911; 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline, call or text 988, free, confidential, 24/7). Kevin Love's quote is from his own published essay; use it as he wrote it.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "love2018", cite: "Love K. Everyone is going through something. The Players' Tribune. March 6, 2018.", url: "https://www.theplayerstribune.com/articles/kevin-love-everyone-is-going-through-something", grade: "first-person essay", found: "external", supports: "Quote: \"Everyone is going through something that we can't see.\"" },
    { id: "gulliver2012", cite: "Gulliver A, Griffiths KM, Christensen H. Barriers and facilitators to mental health help-seeking for young elite athletes: a qualitative study. BMC Psychiatry. 2012;12:157. doi:10.1186/1471-244X-12-157", grade: "qualitative focus groups (15 athletes)", found: "external (cited in Zotero: Schnitker et al. 2020)", supports: "Negative past experiences of help-seeking were a barrier; positive attitudes of others, especially the coach, and an established relationship with a provider were facilitators." },
    { id: "mhfa", cite: "Mental Health First Aid USA (National Council for Mental Wellbeing). Using the 5-step MHFA Action Plan: how ALGEE helps in mental health and substance use challenges. mentalhealthfirstaid.org. Accessed October 4, 2026.", url: "https://mentalhealthfirstaid.org/news/algee-how-mhfa-helps-you-respond-in-crisis-and-non-crisis-situations/", grade: "program's own description", found: "external", supports: "Listen nonjudgmentally: let the person share without interrupting; try to be accepting even if you don't agree. Encourage appropriate professional help." },
    { id: "ncaa2024", cite: "NCAA Sport Science Institute. Mental Health Best Practices: Understanding and Supporting Student-Athlete Mental Health. 2nd ed. NCAA; 2016, revised 2020, updated 2024.", url: "https://ncaaorg.s3.amazonaws.com/ssi/mental/SSI_MentalHealthBestPractices.pdf", grade: "inter-association consensus document", found: "external", supports: "Empathic listening is among suggested coach development topics (p. 5); athletes are referred to licensed mental health providers for evaluation and care (pp. 7, 9)." },
    { id: "dazzi2014", cite: "Dazzi T, Gribble R, Wessely S, Fear NT. Does asking about suicide and related behaviours induce suicidal ideation? What is the evidence? Psychol Med. 2014;44(16):3361-3363. doi:10.1017/S0033291714001299", grade: "review of published studies", found: "external", supports: "No study found a statistically significant increase in suicidal ideation among people asked about suicidal thoughts; acknowledging and talking about suicide may reduce it." },
    { id: "lifeline988", cite: "988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline. 988lifeline.org. Accessed October 4, 2026.", url: "https://988lifeline.org/", grade: "national crisis service", found: "external", supports: "Call or text 988, or chat online; free, confidential, available 24/7/365." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `In 2018, Kevin Love, an NBA All-Star at the time, wrote about having a panic attack during a game. One line from that essay has stuck with a lot of people in sport: "Everyone is going through something that we can't see."

That's true of the athletes in our buildings too. Most of the time we won't see it. But sometimes an athlete will tell one of us. Today is about what we say in that moment.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(3.6, 2.0, 8.98, 2.3, "“Everyone is going through something\nthat we can’t see.”", { font: "light", size: 48, color: "ink", align: "right", lineH: 1.12, role: "lead" });
        s.line(11.58, 4.65, 12.58, 4.65, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.source("Kevin Love, The Players’ Tribune, 2018", { x: 6.58, y: 4.85, w: 6.0, h: 0.4, size: 16, align: "right", valign: "top", refs: ["love2018"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 55,
      notes: `Here's why the first conversation matters so much. When researchers asked young elite athletes what kept them from getting help, one answer was bad past experiences of asking for it. Another was what made it easier: positive attitudes from the people around them, especially their coach.

So if an athlete tries once and gets brushed off, told to toughen up, or met with panic, they may not try again. If it goes well, the next step gets easier.

It was a small study, fifteen athletes, but it matches what clinicians tell us: the person they tell first matters.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The first conversation decides whether there’s a second.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 1.55, size: 44 });
        s.line(0.75, 3.15, 12.58, 3.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.4, 11.4, 1.9, "Young athletes said bad experiences of asking for help kept them quiet, and a coach’s positive attitude made it easier to speak up.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.2 });
        s.line(0.75, 5.55, 12.58, 5.55, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.source("Gulliver, Griffiths & Christensen 2012: focus groups with 15 young elite athletes", { y: 5.95, w: 9.5, size: 12, refs: ["gulliver2012"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `So what helps? Mostly, listening before fixing.

On the left are things most of us have said, with good intentions: toughen up, you'll be fine, stay positive. They're meant to help, but they can tell the athlete to stop talking.

On the right is what helps more. Thank them for telling you. Name that it sounds hard. Ask what would help, instead of guessing. Mental Health First Aid calls this listening nonjudgmentally: let them talk without interrupting, and accept what they say even if you'd see it differently.

These are example phrases written for this deck, not a script. The point is the direction: less fixing, more listening.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Listen first. Fixing can wait.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.75, size: 40 });
        s.label("What we tend to say", { x: 0.75, y: 2.35, w: 4.5 });
        s.label("What helps more", { x: 6.4, y: 2.35, w: 4.5, color: "accent" });
        const pairs = [
          ["“Toughen up.”", "“Thanks for telling me.”"],
          ["“You’ll be fine.”", "“That sounds really hard.”"],
          ["“Just stay positive.”", "“What would help right now?”"],
        ];
        pairs.forEach(([a, b], i) => {
          const y = 2.8 + i * 0.95;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.2, 4.6, 0.6, a, { font: "light", size: 26, color: "muted" });
          s.line(5.25, y + 0.47, 5.95, y + 0.47, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, arrow: "end" });
          s.text(6.4, y + 0.18, 6.18, 0.6, b, { font: "medium", size: 28, color: "accent" });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 5.65, 12.58, 5.65, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.source("Example phrases written for this deck · Listening without judgment: MHFA action plan; NCAA Mental Health Best Practices 2024", { y: 6.2, w: 11.8, refs: ["mhfa", "ncaa2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `If you're worried about an athlete, do two things.

First, ask directly. If you're worried about suicide, it's OK to use the word: "Are you thinking about suicide?" Many of us fear that asking will put the idea in someone's head. A review of the research found that asking doesn't increase suicidal thinking, and talking about it may help.

Second, walk them to help. Don't just hand over a phone number; offer to go with them to Baylor's licensed mental health staff, who take it from there.

And if someone may be in immediate danger, call 911. The 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is free, confidential and open around the clock: call or text 988.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("If you’re worried, ask directly. Then walk them to help.", { y: 1.6, w: 11.0, h: 1.75, size: 44 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.75, 10.5, 0.95, "Asking about suicide doesn’t plant the idea. Baylor’s licensed mental health staff take it from there.", { font: "light", size: 24, color: "ink", lineH: 1.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.15, 10.5, 0.45, "Immediate danger: call 911. Crisis Lifeline: call or text 988.", { font: "medium", size: 20, color: "accent" });
        s.source("Dazzi et al., Psychol Med, 2014 · 988lifeline.org", { y: 6.0, w: 8, size: 12, refs: ["dazzi2014", "lifeline988"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The one line to take away: listen first, then connect. We don't have to be the counselor. We have to be someone it went well with.

So here's the question, and it's worth actually answering in your head: if an athlete told you today that they were struggling, what would your first sentence be?

If people are willing, have a few share. Hearing each other's first sentences is often the most useful part of this.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.75, 11.0, 3.4, "If an athlete told you today, what would your first sentence be?", { font: "medium", size: 54, color: "accent", lineH: 1.12, role: "headline" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

